    <briefdescription>
<para>This header is the ASU firmware CONTRACT for the lower-level (native) ML-KEM entry points exposed by the liboqs &quot;mlkem-native&quot; backend. </para>    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>liboqs&apos;s generic OQS_KEM API reaches these same functions indirectly, through a descriptor object that OQS_KEM_new() looks up by algorithm name and allocates on the heap. The firmware calls the native symbols directly instead, so that the ML-KEM path needs no allocator, and so that the name-keyed registry, which references every algorithm liboqs was built with, does not defeat <ndash/>gc-sections and anchor unused backends.</para><para>These prototypes MUST match the symbols exported by liboqs.a exactly. The exact link-level names were verified via <computeroutput>nm liboqs.a</computeroutput> and follow the pattern: PQCP_MLKEM_NATIVE_MLKEM{512|768|1024}_C_&lt;function&gt;</para><para>The build of liboqs used here does NOT define MLK_CONFIG_CONTEXT_PARAMETER, therefore the functions have NO trailing application-context argument. Defining it would add that argument without changing a single symbol name, so the mismatch would link silently. build_liboqs.sh checks for exactly that before it builds: it compiles these declarations alongside mlkem-native&apos;s own, where a disagreement is a compile error. Keep the two in step, and see README.liboqs.md section 2 if that check fires.</para><para>NOTE: The liboqs drop agent must ensure these symbols are exported (not stripped) from liboqs.a. This header is the single source of truth; the drop must conform to it.</para><para><preformatted>
MODIFICATION HISTORY:</preformatted></para><para><preformatted>Ver   Who  Date     Changes
<hruler/>

1.0   ss   08/20/26 Initial release</preformatted></para><para><preformatted></preformatted> </para>    </detaileddescription>
